! I-Box 4 diagnostic program (docs/jb_ax4b_ibox-4.pdf p.159, routine 150
! "----1250---CB AND CBR TEST", the IBR96-IBR99 block) -- the "CBR"
! mnemonic (Count, Branch, and Refill: v=00/refill=1/branch-if-nonzero),
! second slice of the CB/CBR family after ibox4_cb_count_branch.strap
! (see that file's header and docs/archival_inventory.md for how this
! candidate family was chosen and section 138 rejected). Exercises a
! code path nothing else in this suite touches: StretchExecute.bsv's
! CountBranch arm's OWN refill mechanism (`doRefill = (cb.refill == 1)
! && (x1.count == 0)`, then `needsRead = tagged Valid zeroExtend(x1.
! refill)` / `unpackIndexWord(prefetch[0])`) -- distinct from
! xw_refill.strap's refill-on-count-zero, which goes through ImmIndex's
! V+ICR/progressive-indexing path instead, a different arm entirely.
!
! Source transcription note: routine 150's own IBR96-IBR99 loads X1
! from an XW literal (IBRA9: VALUE=self-referential, COUNT=3,
! REFILL=IBRA10, a second XW literal at COUNT=037777 octal -- large
! enough it can't spuriously reach zero -- holding the word CBR should
! refill from) and executes CBR three times: the first two decrement a
! nonzero count and branch, WITHOUT refilling ("COUNT 3 TO 2,BR,NO
! REFILL" / "COUNT 2 TO 1,BR,NO REFILL", the source's own comments);
! the third decrements count to zero, does NOT branch (bz=0, branch-
! -if-nonzero, and count is now zero), and DOES refill ("COUNT 1 TO
! 0,NO BR, REFILL"). Reproduced here with simplified literals (VALUE=
! 111/222 instead of address-derived values, COUNT=3/16383 instead of
! octal 037777) since the specific numbers aren't the point -- the
! sequencing (2 branches with no refill, then 1 non-branch WITH
! refill, loading the refill target's contents verbatim) is.
!
! Pass/fail substitution, same principle as WEN/WEN2/VEN and
! ibox4_cb_count_branch.strap: the source's own signaling (SX-store +
! self-modifying-code branch into the stored value, "TERM TEST"/error-
! table branches) is replaced with externally-checkable state. Unlike
! the plain-CB slice, this test needs a MID-sequence snapshot (X1's
! state after the 2nd CBR, before the 3rd fires refill) that the
! testbench -- which only reads final register state after halt --
! can't see directly. Solved via this project's own memory-mapped
! index-register convention (StretchTypes.bsv addrIndexBase=16, i.e.
! index register N is also plain memory word 16+N, confirmed real --
! not just an assembler convenience -- by reading StretchStorage.bsv's
! readWord/writeWord: addresses 16-31 alias directly into the same
! `xreg` array readIndex/writeIndex use): `LX,$5,$X1` loads X5 from
! word addrIndex(1), i.e. copies X1's CURRENT full packed state into
! X5 at that exact program point, unaffected by X1's later changes.
! Same idiom for the final post-refill snapshot into X6.
!
! Checks:
!   X2/X3 = pass marker (branch taken) for CBR calls 1 and 2.
!   X4 = fail marker (should stay 0): set only if call 3 wrongly
!        branches when count reaches zero.
!   X5 = snapshot after call 2, before call 3 fires refill -- must
!        equal CTL's own packed value with count=1 (value/refill
!        fields unchanged) -- this is what catches an early/spurious
!        refill, the main way a refill-guard bug would show up.
!   X6 = snapshot after call 3 -- must equal REFILLTGT's packed
!        literal verbatim (an unconditional load, not a merge --
!        StretchExecute.bsv's own comment: `unpackIndexWord(prefetch
!        [0])`), proving refill read the RIGHT word, not merely a
!        word that happens to satisfy some weaker check.
!
! Expected hex values for X1/X5/X6-style checks are computed by
! running this project's own assembler (strap_asm.assemble) and
! deriving the packed IndexWord bits from its symbol table, the same
! oracle-derivation approach as figure7_progressive_indexing.strap --
! not hand-computed, since REFILLTGT's word address depends on the
! assembled layout.
!
! Verified non-vacuous with a refill-specific bug injection (a branch-
! -sense flip, as used for the plain-CB slice, would NOT exercise this
! code at all): temporarily dropped CountBranch's own refill guard in
! StretchExecute.bsv (`Bool doRefill = (cb.refill == 1) && (x1.count ==
! 0);` -> `Bool doRefill = (cb.refill == 1);`, refilling on every CBR
! regardless of count), rebuilt, and re-ran. ACTUAL result (not what
! was predicted before running it -- see the correction this comment
! itself represents): X2/X3 PASS, X4/X5/X6 all FAIL. The failure is
! bigger than "X5 alone catches it": because the bug makes call 1
! request a refill too, X1 gets overwritten by REFILLTGT's own
! contents (refill field 0) after call 1 already, so calls 2 and 3
! then try to refill from word 0 instead of REFILLTGT -- corrupting
! not just X5 (no-refill-yet, wrongly already refilled) but also X6
! (refilled from the wrong word) and even X4 (call 3's branch decision
! itself flips, since it's now operating on a corrupted count). X2/X3
! stay green because calls 1 and 2 still see a nonzero count going in
! regardless of the extra refill traffic. Reverted and confirmed
! `git diff` on StretchExecute.bsv is empty before committing.

START   LX, $1, CTL         !X1 = XW literal VALUE=111,COUNT=3,REFILL=REFILLTGT
        LVI, $2, 0.0        !X2 = 0 (fail marker: call 1 branch not taken)
        LVI, $3, 0.0        !X3 = 0 (fail marker: call 2 branch not taken)
        LVI, $4, 0.0        !X4 = 0 (fail marker: call 3 branch WAS taken -- bug)

        CBR, $1, S1OK       !IBR97 -COUNT 3 TO 2, BR, NO REFILL
        B, S1DONE
S1OK    LVI, $2, 100
S1DONE  CBR, $1, S2OK       !IBR98 -COUNT 2 TO 1, BR, NO REFILL
        B, S2DONE
S2OK    LVI, $3, 100
S2DONE  LX, $5, $X1         !snapshot X1 (count=1, no refill yet) into X5
        CBR, $1, S3BUG      !IBR99 -COUNT 1 TO 0, NO BR, REFILL
        B, DONE
S3BUG   LVI, $4, 100        !reached only if the branch wrongly fired at count=0

DONE    LX, $6, $X1         !snapshot X1's final (post-refill) state into X6
        BEW, $              !halt

CTL       XW, 111, 3, REFILLTGT   !VALUE=111,COUNT=3,REFILL=word(REFILLTGT)
REFILLTGT XW, 222, 16383, 0       !distinctive VALUE/COUNT (mirrors the
                                  !source's own "037777, deliberately huge"
                                  !idea, though nothing decrements this
                                  !literal's own count in this slice)

          END, START
